# Frontend Spec

## Scope

React UI architecture, shared state, route-level pages, CSS system, store/cart,
and the Bible viewer/browser-narration contracts.

## Load when

Changing React components, page routes, atoms/hooks, CSS, cart/store UI, Bible
navigation/narration, WebSocket consumers, or page-builder UX.

## Owns

- App shell/routes: `frontend/src/App.tsx`, `frontend/src/pages/routes.tsx`,
  `frontend/src/pages/Layout.tsx`
- State: `frontend/src/atoms/*`
- API utilities: `frontend/src/utils/api.ts`
- WebSocket singleton: `frontend/src/hooks/useWebSocketSync.ts`
- Design tokens/utilities: `frontend/src/index.css`, `frontend/src/utilities.css`
- Shared URL-backed media state: `frontend/src/components/ui/MediaPlaceholder.tsx`
- Deferred rendering and loading UI: `frontend/src/components/ui/DeferredRender.tsx`,
  `frontend/src/components/ui/Skeleton.tsx`,
  `frontend/src/components/ui/AsyncBoundary.tsx`
- Domain pages/components: `src/pages/*`, `src/components/*`, `src/styles/*`
- Bible UI/data: `src/pages/kjv/*`, `src/components/bible/*`,
  `src/atoms/bible.ts`
- Documentation UI/data: `src/pages/documentation/*`,
  `src/components/documentation/*`, `src/atoms/documentation.ts`

## Architecture Contracts

- React + TypeScript + Vite.
- Jotai atoms are the cross-page state boundary.
- REST is used for initial reads and mutations; mutable shared state converges
  through WebSocket pushes into atoms.
- REST is also the default API transport. The experimental WebSocket REST bridge
  is used only when `VITE_WS_API_BRIDGE_ENABLED=true` and a socket is open;
  disconnect rejects all pending requests, responses resolve only matching
  random request IDs, and read coalescing includes the effective auth headers.
- `useWebSocketSync` is mounted by `Layout.tsx` and can be called by consumers
  for `subscribe`/`unsubscribe`; it must remain a singleton per browser context.
- Route pages live under `src/pages`; reusable domain UI lives under
  `src/components`; shared CSS tokens live only in `src/index.css`.
- `components/ui/` is the only foundation primitive owner. `Button`, `Select`,
  `Checkbox`, `Surface`, `ComposerInput`, `TextField`/`TextArea`, `GlassMenu`,
  `PromptContainer`, and shared feedback components own their common semantics,
  interaction states, and token CSS. `components/form/` is the Formik adapter
  layer. Files under `components/input/` are deprecated direct re-exports only;
  they contain no implementation or CSS authority and exist solely for the
  migration window.
- Routine application controls use the `Button` action variant; primary and
  secondary variants are reserved for form or major actions. Existing global
  `.btn`, local action recipes, domain cards, and specialized tab systems are
  migration boundaries, not competing foundation APIs. Generic selectable or
  static containers use `Surface`; domain cards may compose it when their data
  and layout contract is genuinely shared. Message composition uses
  `ComposerInput`, while ordinary labeled inputs and textareas use
  `TextField`/`TextArea` or the deliberate Formik adapters.
- `MediaPlaceholder` owns URL-keyed empty, loading, ready, and error presentation
  for image, video, and audio content. Its typed layouts preserve compact,
  fixed-frame, fill, and background geometry while exposing native playback,
  caption, fit, decorative accessibility, and lifecycle behavior.
  `MediaBackground` adds validated cover-video/image and repeated-image behavior
  without interpolating asset URLs into generated style text. Read-only authored
  HTML is sanitized by `RichTextRenderer` and adapts media tags without changing
  saved HTML.
- Forum and documentation authoring share `components/ui/RichTextEditor`; their
  persisted HTML is rendered by the same sanitized `RichTextRenderer`. The
  responsive documentation shell is a GWP component, not a second app shell.
- `npm run check:media-placeholders` rejects unreviewed raw media and dynamic
  image backgrounds. The exact-count allowlist distinguishes migration debt from
  intentional shared primitive, `UserAvatar`, bundled-art, `MediaStream.srcObject`,
  and read-only Markdown-adapter ownership. The enforcement script and focused
  media tests are the continuing verification authority for the completed rollout.
- `MediaPreviewLightbox` owns URL-backed preview portals across uploads, scraped
  media, and commerce. It uses a native modal lifecycle, restores prior focus,
  supports Escape and arrow-key navigation, and keeps controls independent of
  media load success.
- Every eligible `MediaPlaceholder` directly opens its image/video/audio in the
  shared lightbox by pointer or keyboard, including from stable failure states.
  Callers can explicitly opt out for decorative, nested-lightbox, or inert
  preview media; no section-level preview boundary is required.
- Viewport deferral is an explicit mount boundary, not merely a `Suspense`
  fallback. `useViewportActivation` defaults to one-shot activation 200px before
  intersection, accepts custom scroll roots, and renders eagerly when browser
  observation is unavailable. `useIntentActivation` ignores touch/pen hover,
  cancels pointer fly-overs, and activates immediately for keyboard focus or an
  explicit caller-owned touch control.
- Shared skeleton primitives provide token-only text, heading, avatar, media,
  card, table-row, form, chart, and content geometry. One owning live region
  announces loading while decorative bars remain hidden from assistive
  technology; reduced-motion users receive a static treatment.
- `AsyncBoundary` contains lazy/render failures around the smallest useful
  subtree and leaves sibling navigation/actions mounted. Visible async content
  must have a retry path; null Suspense fallbacks are reserved for reviewed
  decorative overlays. Large `TableView` collections use the shared viewport
  hook and skeleton primitive while retaining the existing 25-row threshold.
- The application route boundary uses one pathname-selected skeleton shell for
  page, commerce, documentation/reader, feed, form, profile, table/admin, and
  canvas route families. Primary data surfaces use shared domain-shaped initial
  skeletons while pagination, mutations, empty results, stale refreshes, and
  errors retain separate feedback.
- Route chrome mode is owned by `pages/routeLayoutPolicy.ts` and selected by
  `Layout.tsx`. Routed components must not mutate `layoutModeAtom` during mount
  or cleanup; the atom remains a user preference only for routes outside the
  pathname-owned application set.
- Registered routes use one of these intentional shell families:
  - root browse surfaces use `DirectoryLayout` for their header, toolbar,
    bounded results, and empty state;
  - conventional module child routes use `ModulePageShell`, optionally through
    a domain adapter such as `StorePageShell` or `CommunityModuleShell`;
  - settings and operator routes with persistent section navigation use
    `SideRouteShell`;
  - documentation readers use `DocumentationShell`, while page-builder,
    datasource, flow, stream, clipmaker, visualizer, and temporary-session
    routes retain their purpose-built canvas/editor boundary.
- Conventional module shells expose an outer navigation/meta rail and a stable
  inset content rail. Titles, tabs, directories, detail content, empty/error
  states, and managed forms use the content rail; page-specific classes never
  own the outer shell width or padding. Domain child routes must reuse their
  module adapter so back-link placement cannot drift between siblings.
- Site Settings > Visuals offers a curated Google Fonts list and a validated
  custom family name to `home.manage` users. The public SEO projection supplies
  the selected family; `SiteHead` loads its Google stylesheet and updates the
  shared `--font-sans` token. Empty or unsafe legacy values retain the platform
  font stack and never enter generated CSS.
- Page and thread analytics import only after the analytics action opens. Its
  Suspense fallback mirrors the modal header, stat row, and chart and
  keeps the close action usable. Recharts remains owned by that lazy module;
  the UI exposes aggregates only and never requests visitor IP/identity rows;
  maps, editors, flow/stream canvases, and clipmaker remain isolated by their
  existing route or interaction boundaries.
- `check-loading-boundaries` guards visible null Suspense fallbacks, eager
  analytics/Recharts imports, and the named retired bare initial-loading states.
  Reviewed decorative overlays and compact pagination/mutation indicators are
  explicit exceptions.
- `CustomPages` consumes a content-free browse-summary type independently of
  `PageBuilderDoc`, performs abortable debounced server search, and extends the
  bounded cursor feed from a near-viewport sentinel. List mode never constructs
  preview renderers. Grid cards reserve a 160px domain skeleton; exact preview
  content and the lazy `BlockRenderer` activate only after viewport eligibility
  plus delayed mouse hover, keyboard focus, or an explicit touch action.
- Page previews are inert, reject mismatched page/revision responses, abort on
  revision/card teardown, and use a 24-entry in-memory LRU keyed by page id plus
  `updated_at`. Empty, pending, retryable failure, and ready states retain the
  same thumbnail geometry.
- `sectionRegistry.ts` is the compact renderer-type allowlist. Page section IDs
  and configs retain their observed numeric/string and string/object encodings;
  individual renderers narrow the config fields they consume.
- The default `get-started` landing document is a GWP capability tour built only
  from registered `hero`, `stat_cards`, `feature_grid`, `card_group`, `rich_text`,
  and `cta` renderers. It describes optional capabilities without linking to
  feature-gated or permission-gated destinations and contains no seeded
  interactive records. Tenant-edited variants remain authoritative.
- Every registered or unsupported page section renders through one
  `SectionFrame`. The frame owns shared presentation, collapse, and the single
  toolbar; renderers may portal type-specific actions into it but must not create
  block-local shared shell controls. Renderers retain semantic heading placement
  and inline content editing where their DOM contracts differ.
- `SectionFrame` also owns one-shot child mounting independently from its
  reversible entrance-animation observer. The first section is eager; later
  view/editor sections preload within 280px. Preview sections observe against
  the clipped thumbnail root with no margin, so content below the visible crop
  does not mount. Missing observer support activates safely.
- `SectionSkeleton` covers every canonical and unsupported type with bounded,
  renderer-specific geometry rather than a shared visual-family card. Hero
  retains the real full-bleed 500px desktop/400px mobile composition; ordinary
  cards are text-only, events and galleries own media frames, stats/features
  own icon-led layouts, and CTA, profile, social, rich-text, code, data, and
  interactive sections mirror their distinct hierarchy. It accepts both config
  encodings and bounded `section.items`/field counts without importing
  renderers. The frame owns `aria-busy`; offscreen decorative skeletons do not
  create competing live-region announcements.
- Skeleton composition follows renderer truth, including absence: CTA has no
  fabricated buttons, rich text has no fabricated section heading, social links
  use the exact configured count (including zero), and profile skeletons retain
  the real heading, responsive banner/avatar, links, and checklist layout.
- Editor pointer/focus and collapse expansion activate a deferred section.
  Activation is sticky across moves, content updates, collapse, and active
  drafts; page-key/type-preserving React keys reset it only at page teardown.
- Section colors are inherited or literal; content scale applies to the shared
  inner wrapper. The shared shell also accepts an optional bounded `max_height`;
  when set, only the expanded section content scrolls within that pixel cap,
  while an unset value leaves the section's natural height unconstrained.
  `PageBuilder` always saves the complete authoritative document with the page
  update endpoint.
- `resource_embed` persists a supported resource type plus its source identifier
  or slug. Its compact editor uses resource-type and resource dropdowns backed by
  bounded existing catalog APIs. Read mode mounts the canonical product, thread,
  or documentation route content with an explicit resource identity, rather than
  maintaining summary-card or special embedded-shell renderers. The global app
  header/footer remain outside the section, while source visibility, canonical
  links, mutations, sanitization, realtime behavior, and route loading/error
  states remain owned by the source route.
- Datasource-backed reusable designs use the `SectionPreset` type and
  `/config/section-presets`; `CustomSection` remains a deprecated type alias and
  the backend retains the old route during the compatibility window. The
  datasource editor can reopen and update presets for its datasource through
  that same API. Component groups use explicit up/down ordering, commit typed
  percentage widths after editing, and may bind escaped literal text. Expanding
  a component group switches the datasource editor's left pane from TypeScript
  to a Code/Components-tabbed workspace while the right Preview, Compiled JS,
  and Issues tabs remain visible. Stat components render curated named icons and
  persist a per-item top-left, top-right, left-of-value, or right-of-value icon
  position.
- Datasource editor Run sends the current `.ts` file map and transient env input
  to protected `/config/datasources/preview`. Compilation, environment injection,
  outbound fetches, and execution occur in the backend runner; the browser only
  validates and renders returned rows, diagnostics, compiled output, and bounded
  request telemetry. Browser `eval`/`new Function` is forbidden for datasources.
- Bible reads use only `/bible/kjv/books` and `/bible/kjv/books/{book}`. The
  client coalesces requests, retains the catalog for the page lifetime, and
  bounds its per-book memory cache to eight entries; it never persists corpus
  text or fetches scripture from GitHub at runtime.
- Book payload rendering markers use zero-based Unicode code-point offsets with
  exclusive ends. The reader preserves clean verse strings while showing added
  words in italics, words of Christ with the global red-letter token, and
  paragraph starts as context-flow breaks.

## Destructive UX and Trash

- Authenticated users have one protected `/trash` route built with
  `DirectoryLayout` and grouped `TableView` sections. Each resource group owns
  bounded pagination and per-row Undo controls; the backend response, not
  client role inference, determines which groups and rows are visible.
- `PromptContainer` is the only browser-modal owner. Destructive operations use
  structured `customConfirm`/`confirmDestructiveAction` options with explicit
  title, impact, and action label; permanent file/infrastructure operations say
  that Trash cannot restore them and may require typed confirmation.
- Prompt requests serialize, trap and restore focus, expose an accessible
  `alertdialog`, support Escape/cancel, and settle every queued promise.
  Native `confirm`, `prompt`, `alert`, and unload prompts are forbidden by
  `npm run check:native-dialogs`.

## Service Status UI

- The optional public `/status` and protected `/admin/status` routes exist only
  when `status` is explicitly enabled. They share one responsive token-only page.
- Public UI covers loading, operational, degraded, unavailable, delayed, empty
  incident, populated incident, and retryable error states without rendering
  diagnostic latency or reason codes.
- The operator view adds permission-backed diagnostics and bounded incident
  publication. Backend policy remains authoritative for every write.

## Rewards, Rankings, and Community UI

- Optional `rewards`, `rankings`, and `community` features use lazy routes,
  `DirectoryLayout`, `TableView`, shared media/buttons, sanitized rich text, and
  `ManagedForm`, with separate loading, empty, denied, error, and success states.
- `ModulePageShell` owns the stable content measure, back navigation, and module
  tabs for these route families. Directory headers, toolbars, results, empty
  states, and submodule content align to that same measure rather than inheriting
  an incidental parent width.
- Reward points are never presented as wallet money. Leaderboards render server
  ranks/cursors. Community detail keeps proposal, showcase, and event workflow
  semantics separate and links canonical forum discussion instead of copying it.
- Every community publication owns a real custom page. The community detail
  keeps workflow/media/actions as its submodule chrome, renders the sanitized
  linked `pages.content` document at the full module width, and exposes canonical
  `/page/:slug` and management links to authorized owners/managers. Authoring
  happens in the regular custom-page builder rather than a parallel form editor.
- Every publication owns its canonical forum discussion. Detail always exposes
  the existing thread viewer; the thread owner or `forum.thread-edit` holder also
  receives the existing thread editor route. Community forms do not accept an
  arbitrary discussion ID because the server creates and owns that relationship.
- Community directories and details render server-projected edit/delete controls;
  proposal moderation and vote/event attendance controls appear only when their
  matching capabilities are true. Route-level actions use the same compact
  icon-action controls as forum threads and custom-page directories. Community
  titles use the proportional forum/product detail scale rather than inheriting
  global display typography. The shared create/edit form owns metadata,
  visibility, publication status, kind-specific fields, and canonical discussion.
- `InteractiveActionSection` binds domain-owned actions to the existing
  interactive-section schema, field renderer, validation, submission lifecycle,
  and feedback surface. Proposal votes/transitions and event attendance use this
  adapter; their community endpoints and normalized tables remain authoritative,
  and no response copy is written to `pages.content`. Authored interactive
  sections inside the linked document receive the real page ID and continue to
  use the ordinary page-response API from the embedded community detail view.
- Comfortable `ModulePageShell` content is width-bounded and centered with
  `margin-inline:auto`; wide module content retains the common module measure.
- Reward account boundaries normalize absent provider collections to empty
  arrays. The UI remains functional when a compatible external provider returns
  `null` for an empty grant or redemption collection.
- The public, feature-gated `/community` hub is the compact onboarding and app
  discovery surface. It groups enabled public destinations, signed-in account
  destinations, and permission-derived operator tools without introducing a
  second route registry or treating client visibility as authorization.
- The shared app drawer exposes enabled community workflows, rankings, public
  status, rewards for signed-in users, and the signed-in user's linked-identity
  settings. Disabled features and authenticated-only destinations remain hidden.

