## Bible Viewer and Narration

- Public routes are `/kjv`, the legacy query form
  `/kjv?bt={title}&c={chapter}&v={verse}&o={0|1}`, and the path form
  `/kjv/{bookSlug}/{chapter}/{verse}/{open|closed}`.
- A complete deep link is authoritative over saved selection. Navigation
  preserves the incoming query/path form; narration advances with history
  replacement, and malformed or nonexistent coordinates render an error rather
  than remembered scripture.
- Local storage key `bible.preferences` contains only validated selection,
  narration preference, selected browser voice URI, and at most 500 favorites.
- Narration uses the browser Web Speech API with delayed voice discovery,
  English/default fallback, play/pause/resume/stop, one utterance per verse, and
  continuous cross-chapter advancement. It cancels on reader close, book change,
  disable, or unmount and ignores stale utterance callbacks.
- Reader shortcuts are Left/Right for adjacent verses, `V` for narration,
  Space for play/pause, and Escape for fullscreen exit or narration stop. They
  do not capture navigation/narration events from interactive or editable
  controls.
- Book discovery and the picker use the shared `DirectoryLayout`, `FilterBar`,
  and `TableView`, including persisted grid/table preference, metadata search,
  division filtering, and keyboard-openable rows.
- Fullscreen reader mode is transient route-local UI. It fills the viewport,
  locks background scrolling, preserves picker/favorites dialog layering, and
  exits before reader collapse or through Escape.
- Corpus information presents the embedded source metadata: translation
  description, 1611 first publication, 1769 editorial basis, maintained
  repository origin, last verification date, and Bible SuperSearch KJV module
  6.2.0 verification reference without treating that provider as corpus origin.
- The browser, reader, picker, and favorites dialog use GWP components and
  global CSS tokens; icon-only `Button` controls use the compact 1.75rem
  `size="icon"` contract. No Tailwind, Redux, Radix, or remote scripture
  dependency is part of the feature.

## Store/Cart Frontend Contracts

- `storeCartItemsAtom` persists in local storage as `store.cart`.
- Product/category/catalog atoms persist as `store.products` and `store.categories`.
- Store catalog filters include category, unified product/category/seller search,
  price, rating, sort, and an authenticated `You` owner toggle.
  Price bounds should tolerate inverted min/max input by treating the lower value
  as the minimum.
- Store catalog and order filters use the reusable `FilterBar`; dense order filters
  render through the `TableView` toolbar and cover search, order/payment status,
  and date/total sorting without replacing the live `ordersAtom` source.
- Store chrome may show live wallet balance and pending-order badges fetched from
  `/store/wallet` and `/store/orders`; wallet badges compact thousands as `$1.2k`
  or `$12k`, pending counts include open vendor line status, and loaded user
  orders converge through buyer/manager direct delivery or seller-filtered direct
  delivery. Clients do not create generic order subscriptions.
- Cart page and checkout require an authenticated account.
- Site policies are ordinary public custom pages managed from
  `/form/site/legal`; the hub stores only stable page references and cookie or
  checkout placement selections. Page-builder forms remain optional content and
  are not interpreted as a policy protocol.
- Cookie and checkout acceptance share local storage key
  `skaia.policy-acceptance.v1`. The cookie notice reuses the guest-sandbox banner
  treatment; checkout stays disabled in the client until every selected policy
  is checked. This browser-local state is convenience UX, not a durable receipt.
- Store managers configure checkout selections contextually from the store
  toolbar or the payment card's compact edit action. They also configure the
  notice tone, message, and checkbox template; `{policy}` resolves to the linked
  policy name. Page editor actions live under one `Page tools` drawer tile
  rather than a floating overflow menu or expanded route inventory.
- All four footer variants mirror the cookie-notice policy selection through one
  shared policy strip. Each variant gives the configured client logo a distinct
  decorative background placement; no logo background renders when the client
  has not configured one. Client-authored title, description, links, and tagline
  remain intact, while hardcoded platform product branding is excluded.
- Empty cart state uses the compact storefront empty-state treatment with
  "No products added" copy, not a large CTA card.
- Checkout payload includes `items`, `payment_method_id`, `currency`, delivery
  fields, `billing_info`, and `referral_code`. The cart creates one opaque
  `Idempotency-Key` per canonical payload and preserves it across uncertain or
  failed retries until the payload changes or checkout succeeds.
- Saved checkout details live in local storage key `checkoutSaved`; do not save
  `deliveryTime`.
- Authenticated users may load wallet cards from `/store/wallet/cards` and pay with
  `card_{id}` or `wallet`.
- Successful checkout clears local cart and shows the submitted order view;
  replayed checkout responses converge on the same view without duplicate
  realtime/activity dispatch.
- Order status views surface per-vendor activity in the right summary column with
  compact vendor totals, status, and accept/reject controls for the current
  vendor's own line items.
- Rich inline users in global messages should render all available roles using
  the role catalog colors when only role names are embedded in the message user.
  Inline user cards and profile overlays must share in-flight user/role catalog
  requests so dense chat/message views do not stampede `/users/roles`.
- Own-account settings use the existing routed `ManagedForm` surface for
  profile, security, and linked identities. Identity rows reuse shared buttons
  and Prompt confirmation, expose loading/empty/error/privacy/reverification
  states, and remain responsive without one-off color or action styling.
- Public profiles request the deliberate public identity projection separately;
  an empty or unavailable projection renders nothing and never blocks the core
  profile. The browser never receives provider adapter keys, challenge hashes,
  opaque provider subjects, or provider credentials on this route.
- System inbox messages containing JSON payloads, especially order cards, render
  as compact inline interactive cards that route to the canonical destination
  such as `/store/orders/{id}` and do not echo raw JSON as a caption.
- Global chat join/leave rows use compact colored arrow icons: green right arrow
  for join and red left arrow for leave.
- Presence DEFCON tab includes a compact reset control that clears runtime
  DEFCON state and refreshes telemetry for admins.

## CSS Token Contract

All UI colors, shadows, radii, and fonts must route through global tokens in
`src/index.css`.

| Token family | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `--primary-*`, `--skaia-green-primary` | brand/actions |
| `--bg-*`, `--text-*`, `--border-color` | layout and text |
| `--error-*`, `--success-*`, `--warning-*`, `--info-*` | semantic state |
| `--shadow`, `--shadow-hover`, `--focus-ring` | elevation/focus |
| `--card-*`, `--section-*`, `--panel-*` | repeated containers |
| `--module-*` | activity/module badges |
| `--font-mono` | all monospace text |

## CSS Rules

- Component CSS must not hardcode hex/rgb/rgba colors; use tokens and
  `color-mix(in srgb, var(--token) X%, transparent)` for alpha.
- Do not use `var()` fallbacks.
- Dark mode is `html[data-theme="dark"]`; do not use
  `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`.
- Use shared shadows; add a token before introducing a new shadow.
- Legacy card-like containers may use `.card` during the CSS normalization
  window; new generic static or selectable containers use `Surface`. Section
  containers use `.section`.
- Semantic and form-like containers use `.compact-form-card` for the shared transparent
  flat-glass surface while retaining domain layout classes. Form groups keep comfortable
  separation, labels explain their controls, major actions use `Button`, and icon-only
  actions use the `action-btn`/typed button variants.
- `ManagedForm` is the shared Formik owner for routed editor, authentication, recovery, MFA,
  and access-challenge workflows. Its default editor presentation uses header X/check actions;
  the `grouped` presentation preserves auth-style separated field cards and full-width footer
  submission. Both presentations use `FormSectionIntro` for internal section introductions.
- Checkout retains its parent-owned multi-panel state and separated groups, but uses the same
  `FormSectionIntro` presentation. Only embedded command/confirmation microforms may remain on
  the managed-form audit allowlist.
- Icon-only buttons use `.thread-action-btn` plus size/variant modifiers.
- `!important` is allowed only for third-party overrides, page-builder inline
  alignment overrides, intentional resets, or mobile grid collapse.
- Component CSS should primarily own layout, positioning, overflow, and animation.

## UI Layout Contracts

- List/feed pages use dense, scannable layouts: toolbar first, responsive grid/list
  below, max width around 1400px unless an editor needs more.
- Fixed-format elements need stable dimensions to prevent hover/content layout shift.
- Page-builder editor panes collapse below tablet widths.
- Editable rich text keeps TipTap block add/modify controls in its desktop gutter;
  at phone widths those pointer-oriented controls are hidden and editor content
  uses compact full-width padding consistent with the read-only renderer.
- Guest sandbox mode reveals management controls in the frontend only; backend auth
  remains the authority.
- The collapsed presence launcher is viewport-clamped and remembers its user-selected
  position. Its drag coordinates never affect the expanded mobile overlay or desktop
  split-panel position, and dragging must not activate launcher controls.
- Do not add marketing landing pages when building app functionality.

